  "verdict": {
    "outcome": "O10",
    "direction": "worsens",
    "magnitude": "minor",
    "time_horizon": "this-parliament",
    "confidence": "moderate",
    "plain": "The policy mandates a specific parenting arrangement (50/50 shared care) and gives grandparents enforceable access rights, both of which override parents' autonomous choices about their own family life. The 'where appropriate' qualifier limits but does not remove the coercive element.",
    "biggest_unknown": "How broadly courts interpret 'where appropriate' will determine how often the 50/50 mandate is imposed against parental preference \u2014 a narrow reading could make the liberty cost negligible.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy mandates 50/50 shared parental care where appropriate and ensures grandparents' rights of access.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "mandate 50/50 shared parental care where appropriate, and ensure rights of access for grandparents"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Current law in England and Wales does not impose any presumption of 50/50 shared care; courts are guided by the child's welfare as the paramount consideration.",
        "evidence_id": "E19",
        "quote": "In England and Wales, there is no automatic legal right or presumption of 50/50 shared care after separation"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "All current decisions on children's arrangements are made under the Children Act 1989 with the child's welfare as paramount, not a fixed parental-time presumption.",
        "evidence_id": "E20",
        "quote": "All decisions regarding children's arrangements are guided by the Children Act 1989, with the child's welfare being the paramount consideration"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Parliament has explicitly chosen not to enact a presumption of 50/50 shared care despite having the opportunity to do so.",
        "evidence_id": "E22",
        "quote": "the UK Parliament has explicitly *not* enacted a presumption of 50/50 shared care"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Grandparents currently have no automatic legal right to see their grandchildren and must seek the court's permission before even applying for a contact order.",
        "evidence_id": "E29",
        "quote": "grandparents do not have an automatic legal right to see their grandchildren"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "The two-stage court process for grandparents (leave to apply, then full application) is described as lengthy, costly, and emotionally difficult.",
        "evidence_id": "E31",
        "quote": "This two-stage process can be lengthy, costly, and emotionally difficult"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "High parental conflict in a mandated shared-care arrangement can expose children to ongoing stress.",
        "evidence_id": "E24",
        "quote": "high parental conflict can expose children to ongoing stress in a shared care arrangement"
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "O10 is the home for negative liberty \u2014 freedom from state coercion over private choices, including family arrangements. This policy introduces two distinct coercive elements. First, mandating 50/50 shared parental care displaces parental autonomy: where parents would otherwise reach a different arrangement by agreement or under a welfare-first judicial assessment, the state imposes a numerical default. The evidence confirms Parliament has deliberately refrained from this step precisely because a presumption of equal time is not the same as the welfare-centred assessment current law requires. The 'where appropriate' qualifier introduces judicial discretion, which limits but does not eliminate the coercive effect \u2014 it shifts the burden onto parents who must argue their circumstances away from the default. Second, guaranteeing grandparents' rights of access creates a new enforceable claim on parents' choices about who may have contact with their child. Currently parents can deny access without court intervention; this policy converts grandparental access into a right that can be litigated against them. Both measures enlarge the state's reach into intimate family decisions. The special court division is largely procedural and, on its own, would not worsen liberty \u2014 improved enforcement of existing obligations is not a new coercive power. The net effect is a worsening of parental autonomy through two new state-backed mandates. The magnitude is minor rather than moderate because the 50/50 clause is expressly qualified and courts already have powers to order contact; these reforms expand rather than create judicial involvement. Confidence is moderate because the real-world coercive impact depends entirely on how courts construe 'where appropriate', which the policy text does not define.",
